Standard_MMgrOpt: no mmap() on wasm32-wasip1, and the emulation that would supply one does not fit.

THE DECISION, stated because this is the one file of the eight that a build flag could have
answered. Recompiled with -D_WASI_EMULATED_MMAN and no source change at all, it produces ZERO
errors; that is #2172's measurement and it is correct. It is patched anyway, for two reasons:

  1. The define makes it compile and does not make it work. wasi-libc's emulated mmap() serves
     anonymous private mappings out of malloc() and refuses everything else. This file's generic
     non-Windows branch defines MMAP_FLAGS as (MAP_PRIVATE), with no MAP_ANON, and passes
     myMMap == -1 as the file descriptor, so every call returns MAP_FAILED. AllocMemory() then
     calls Purge(false) and throws Standard_OutOfMemory, on every large block, for as long as
     MMGT_OPT=1 is set in the host environment. Scripts/repro/2173/run.sh compiles, links and RUNS
     that exact call under the pinned toolchain rather than arguing it from the headers.
  2. The other half of the define is -lwasi-emulated-mman, a link-time flag nothing can verify
     until the full library links (#2174). docs/WASI_GUARD_SITES.md reserves adding an emulation
     flag to the build script for evidence, and evidence that covers only the compile half is not
     the evidence it asks for.

Two of the four sites EXTEND the condition already there:

  * Initialize()'s `#if defined(IRIX) || ... || defined(__ANDROID__) ... #else myMMap = -1;` chain
    gains an `#elif defined(__wasi__)` arm.
  * FreeMemory()'s `#ifndef _WIN32` around the aSize parameter name becomes
    `#if !defined(_WIN32) && !defined(__wasi__)`, so the parameter stays unnamed and unused rather
    than named and unused.

Two are new conditions, because none was available: the `#include <sys/mman.h>` sits with
`#include <unistd.h>` under one `#ifndef _WIN32` and WASI needs the second of the two, and the
mapped arms of AllocMemory() and FreeMemory() are `#ifndef _WIN32` / `#else` pairs with no third
arm to widen.

WHAT THE WASI BRANCH RETURNS: Initialize() leaves myMMap at 0. That is not a new state invented for
this platform: it is the state the class is in on every platform where MMGT_MMAP is off, and the
one Initialize() already forces for itself when getpagesize() returns 0. AllocMemory() and
FreeMemory() then take their calloc/malloc and free() paths, which are complete implementations
rather than fallbacks, and every caller gets ordinary heap memory with the same alignment,
clearing and Purge()-on-failure behaviour as before. The two mapped branches become unreachable and
are compiled to a raise and a no-op rather than deleted, so a later change that makes one reachable
fails loudly instead of returning a null block.

Worth recording, because it bounds the blast radius: this build never instantiates the class at
all. OCCT selects Standard_MMgrOpt only under OCCT_MMGT_OPT_FLEXIBLE, which
src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/CMakeLists.txt defines only when USE_MMGR_TYPE is FLEXIBLE, and
Scripts/build-occt-wasm.sh leaves that at OCCT's NATIVE default. The patch is written to be correct
under FLEXIBLE, not to be correct only in this configuration.

Issue: https://github.com/SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift/issues/2173

diff --git a/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/Standard/Standard_MMgrOpt.cxx b/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/Standard/Standard_MMgrOpt.cxx
index 5e2d64d1..79d820eb 100644
--- a/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/Standard/Standard_MMgrOpt.cxx
+++ b/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/Standard/Standard_MMgrOpt.cxx
@@ -27,7 +27,9 @@
 
 #ifndef _WIN32
   #include <unistd.h>
-  #include <sys/mman.h> /* mmap() */
+  #ifndef __wasi__
+    #include <sys/mman.h> /* mmap() */
+  #endif
 #endif
 
 #include <fcntl.h>
@@ -262,6 +264,10 @@ void Standard_MMgrOpt::Initialize()
     {
       perror("ERR_MMAP_FAIL");
     }
+#elif defined(__wasi__)
+    // wasip1 has no mmap(); leave myMMap at 0 so that AllocMemory() and FreeMemory() take the
+    // malloc/free path this class already implements for every platform where mapping is off
+    myMMap = 0;
 #else
     myMMap = -1;
 #endif
@@ -755,7 +761,13 @@ retry:
   // if MMap option is ON, allocate using memory mapped files
   if (myMMap)
   {
-#ifndef _WIN32
+#if defined(__wasi__)
+
+    // unreachable: Initialize() leaves myMMap at 0 on wasip1, which has no mmap(). Raising rather
+    // than returning null keeps a future reachable path loud instead of silently allocating zero.
+    throw Standard_OutOfMemory("Standard_MMgrOpt::AllocMemory(): no mmap() on wasm32-wasip1");
+
+#elif !defined(_WIN32)
 
     // align size to page size
     const size_t AlignedSize = PAGE_ALIGN(Size, myPageSize);
@@ -859,7 +871,7 @@ retry:
 
 void Standard_MMgrOpt::FreeMemory(void* aBlock,
                                   const size_t
-#ifndef _WIN32
+#if !defined(_WIN32) && !defined(__wasi__)
                                     aSize
 #endif
 )
@@ -867,7 +879,10 @@ void Standard_MMgrOpt::FreeMemory(void* aBlock,
   // release memory (either free or unmap)
   if (myMMap)
   {
-#ifndef _WIN32
+#if defined(__wasi__)
+    // unreachable: AllocMemory() never returns a mapped block on wasip1, see there
+    (void)aBlock;
+#elif !defined(_WIN32)
     // align size to page size, just the same as in AllocMemory()
     const size_t AlignedSize = PAGE_ALIGN(aSize, myPageSize);
     munmap((char*)aBlock, AlignedSize);
